src/cadrumo/_data/corpus/aeat_official/** -text

# The normatives corpus is byte-exact legal evidence too: every source declares
# its own sha256 and byte count, and the registry validator compares them
# against the bytes on disk. Line-ending translation on checkout changes both,
# which broke `boe-modelo-194-form-layout` -- its blob is 103,935 bytes and its
# working copy was 104,069, exactly the 134 CRLF pairs git had introduced.
src/cadrumo/_data/corpus/normatives/** -text

# Every corpus tree, including ones added later. The two rules above name the
# trees that had already been bitten; this one closes the class. Corpus files
# are byte-exact evidence whose sha256 and byte count are declared in the
# registry and checked against disk, so any line-ending translation makes a
# source fail validation -- and a tree whose sources happen not to declare a
# byte count would drift silently instead of failing. eu_official's four HTML
# sources were `text=auto` when this was written and matched only by luck.
src/cadrumo/_data/corpus/** -text

# Binary content, by extension rather than by location.
#
# `* text=auto` above asks git to GUESS which files are text and normalise
# their line endings. The guess is a heuristic over the first few kilobytes,
# and it is wrong often enough to matter here: a PDF or workbook whose bytes
# happen to look textual gets its CRLF pairs rewritten on checkout or on a
# renormalise, which silently changes content-addressed evidence. Several of
# these fixtures declare their own sha256 in a sidecar and are graded against
# it, so a rewritten byte is not a cosmetic difference -- it invalidates the
# artefact and whatever conclusion was drawn from it.
#
# The path rule above covers one corpus directory. These cover the rest of
# the repository's binary inventory wherever it lives: AEAT specimens and
# manual annexes, official workbooks, evidence-corpus images, and shipped
# fonts. `binary` is `-text -diff`, so git neither rewrites the bytes nor
# tries to render a textual diff of them.
*.pdf binary
*.xls binary
*.xlsx binary
*.xlsm binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.zip binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
